 I'm here to help you resolve CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) errors in Next.js when working with WordPress as your backend. CORS is a security feature that restricts web pages from making requests to a different domain than the one that served the web page. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you get started:

1. **Enable CORS on WordPress:**
   To enable CORS on your WordPress site, you need to add some headers to your `functions.php` file or use a plugin like "WP REST API CORS." Adding these headers will allow your Next.js app to make requests to your WordPress backend without encountering CORS errors.

   For the plugin method, install and activate the plugin, then go to Settings > REST > Permalinks and enter the following URLs in the allowed domains field:

   ```
   *.yournextjsapp.com
   123.456.789.0
   localhost
   ```

   Replace `*.yournextjsapp.com` with your actual Next.js app domain and `123.456.789.0` with your server IP address if applicable.

   If you prefer using the functions.php method, add the following code snippet to your `functions.php` file:

   ```php
   function add_cors_headers() {
       header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *.yournextjsapp.com');
       header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE');
       header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization');
   }
   add_action('init', 'add_cors_headers');
   ```

   Replace `*.yournextjsapp.com` with your actual Next.js app domain.

2. **Set up custom headers in Next.js:**
   In your Next.js project, create a new file called `next-env.d.ts` in the root directory and add the following content:

   ```typescript
   declare global {
     namespace NodeJS {
       interface ProcessEnv {
         NEXT_PUBLIC_CORS_ORIGIN: string;
       }

